1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,300
A lot of people hear stable job

2
00:00:02,533 --> 00:00:04,000
and their first reaction is no ambition

3
00:00:04,333 --> 00:00:06,800
But Chinese people call a stable job a rice bowl

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,800
and those two words are actually very real

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,266
A rice bowl is not about dreams

6
00:00:10,266 --> 00:00:11,166
not about passion

7
00:00:11,366 --> 00:00:12,633
It only asks one thing

8
00:00:12,766 --> 00:00:14,366
will there still be food tomorrow

9
00:00:14,366 --> 00:00:16,400
So this video makes just one point

10
00:00:16,566 --> 00:00:17,900
stability is not falling behind

11
00:00:18,166 --> 00:00:19,633
What a rice bowl really stands for

12
00:00:19,666 --> 00:00:21,500
is predictable cash flow for your life

13
00:00:21,666 --> 00:00:23,433
The question is not whether you want a rice bowl

14
00:00:23,433 --> 00:00:24,100
it is

15
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:25,266
whether what you hold is a floor

16
00:00:25,266 --> 00:00:26,200
or a ceiling

17
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,100
First picture a scene you know well

18
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:29,566
going home for the New Year dinner

19
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:30,666
and a relative asks you

20
00:00:30,766 --> 00:00:32,100
is your job stable now

21
00:00:32,166 --> 00:00:33,433
do you have full social insurance

22
00:00:33,566 --> 00:00:34,700
is the company reliable

23
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:35,866
When you hear these questions

24
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:37,200
you might feel a little annoyed

25
00:00:37,466 --> 00:00:38,700
why ask all this again

26
00:00:38,866 --> 00:00:40,466
why not ask whether I like it

27
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:41,833
But look at it another way

28
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:43,000
when they ask about stability

29
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,400
they are not just worried about face

30
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:45,833
often they are really asking

31
00:00:45,866 --> 00:00:47,500
will you suddenly lose your income

32
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,033
if you get sick are you covered

33
00:00:49,166 --> 00:00:50,866
can you keep paying rent or mortgage

34
00:00:50,933 --> 00:00:52,466
when the family suddenly needs money

35
00:00:52,466 --> 00:00:54,000
is there a steady source

36
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,766
That is the weight of the word rice bowl

37
00:00:55,766 --> 00:00:57,433
The bowl does not hold career ideals

38
00:00:57,566 --> 00:00:59,800
it holds an ordinary family's guard against uncertainty

39
00:00:59,966 --> 00:01:01,300
Young people talk about possibilities

40
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:02,966
parents talk about the bottom line of life

41
00:01:03,166 --> 00:01:04,700
Neither side is completely wrong

42
00:01:04,966 --> 00:01:06,700
they just look at different lengths of time

43
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:08,266
Put rice bowl into plain words

44
00:01:08,366 --> 00:01:10,266
and it simply means predictable cash flow

45
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:11,833
money comes in every month

46
00:01:11,933 --> 00:01:13,166
at a roughly fixed time

47
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:14,433
in a roughly known amount

48
00:01:14,533 --> 00:01:15,700
with social and medical insurance unbroken

49
00:01:15,866 --> 00:01:17,133
You can plan for rent

50
00:01:17,133 --> 00:01:18,066
plan for food

51
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:19,433
plan for the old and the kids

52
00:01:19,466 --> 00:01:20,733
plan for a little savings

53
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:22,266
These things do not sound cool

54
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:24,266
but they are the chassis your life runs on

55
00:01:24,333 --> 00:01:26,200
Many people say instability is freedom

56
00:01:26,366 --> 00:01:27,766
that is only half true

57
00:01:27,766 --> 00:01:28,900
If you have savings

58
00:01:28,966 --> 00:01:29,800
have skills

59
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:30,966
have family support

60
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:32,466
of course you can take more risks

61
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:34,233
But if someone's income stops

62
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:35,566
and the whole family tenses up

63
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:37,366
then instability is not freedom

64
00:01:37,466 --> 00:01:39,266
it is doing the math all over again every day

65
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:41,400
So do not be quick to mock people for wanting stability

66
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:43,100
Some stability is not a lack of dreams

67
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:44,566
it is holding life up first

68
00:01:44,566 --> 00:01:46,066
But the rice bowl has another side

69
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:47,266
A good rice bowl

70
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:48,700
should leave you, once you are full

71
00:01:48,733 --> 00:01:49,900
still with energy to learn

72
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:51,500
with room to care for your body

73
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,866
with space to build up skills

74
00:01:52,866 --> 00:01:54,266
But a bad rice bowl

75
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:55,966
leaves you with just enough to get by

76
00:01:55,966 --> 00:01:56,766
afraid to move

77
00:01:56,766 --> 00:01:57,566
afraid to learn

78
00:01:57,666 --> 00:01:59,200
afraid to admit you do not like it

79
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:00,833
At that point it is no longer just a floor

80
00:02:00,933 --> 00:02:02,366
it starts to become a ceiling

81
00:02:02,366 --> 00:02:03,433
For the sake of stability

82
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,166
you slowly hand your judgment to your employer

83
00:02:05,166 --> 00:02:06,066
for the sake of stability

84
00:02:06,066 --> 00:02:07,300
you stop updating your skills

85
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:08,200
for the sake of stability

86
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,766
you bet all your security on one pay slip

87
00:02:10,766 --> 00:02:12,066
A few years later you find

88
00:02:12,166 --> 00:02:13,433
it is not that you do not want to change

89
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:15,166
but that you no longer know how

90
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:17,100
So the question is not whether stability is good

91
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:18,400
what you should really ask is

92
00:02:18,466 --> 00:02:20,666
does this stability give you more choices

93
00:02:20,766 --> 00:02:22,400
or make you more and more unable to leave it

94
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:24,166
So how does an ordinary person use this

95
00:02:24,166 --> 00:02:24,900
First

96
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:26,700
do not mock yourself for needing security

97
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:27,633
people need to eat

98
00:02:27,733 --> 00:02:28,566
need to see a doctor

99
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:29,666
need to care for family

100
00:02:29,966 --> 00:02:30,800
of course you need a bottom line

101
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:32,166
Admitting you need a rice bowl

102
00:02:32,166 --> 00:02:32,866
is nothing to be ashamed of

103
00:02:32,866 --> 00:02:33,400
Second

104
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,066
do not only look at whether your employer is stable

105
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:36,600
also look at whether you yourself are stable

106
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:37,800
are your skills stable

107
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:39,100
are your savings stable

108
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:40,333
is your body stable

109
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:41,766
is your ability to learn stable

110
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:43,100
If only the employer is stable

111
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:44,133
and you are not

112
00:02:44,133 --> 00:02:45,600
then that stability is very thin

113
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:46,266
Third

114
00:02:46,266 --> 00:02:47,733
build yourself a small rice bowl

115
00:02:47,733 --> 00:02:48,966
not starting a business right away

116
00:02:48,966 --> 00:02:50,200
nor leaving immediately

117
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,566
but outside your main job

118
00:02:51,566 --> 00:02:53,266
slowly building skills you can carry anywhere

119
00:02:53,266 --> 00:02:53,933
being able to write

120
00:02:53,933 --> 00:02:54,500
to speak

121
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:55,400
to make a plan

122
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:56,266
to edit video

123
00:02:56,333 --> 00:02:57,800
to turn experience into work

124
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:00,200
At first these may not earn much money

125
00:03:00,266 --> 00:03:01,400
but they remind you

126
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:02,800
I am not just a position

127
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:04,300
I have other ways to earn my meal

128
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:05,533
A truly good rice bowl

129
00:03:05,533 --> 00:03:07,100
does not pin you in place

130
00:03:07,133 --> 00:03:08,600
it lets you, once you are full

131
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:09,866
have the energy to go a little further

132
00:03:09,866 --> 00:03:11,600
So the best state for an ordinary person

133
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:13,133
is not mocking stability

134
00:03:13,133 --> 00:03:14,300
while fearing change

135
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:15,600
but honestly admitting

136
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:16,700
I need a bottom line

137
00:03:16,733 --> 00:03:17,800
and I also need possibilities

138
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:18,900
I need a bowl

139
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,666
but I cannot let it become my whole life

140
00:03:20,666 --> 00:03:22,366
Next time we will keep talking about red envelopes

141
00:03:22,366 --> 00:03:24,566
why getting a red envelope as a kid felt so happy

142
00:03:24,566 --> 00:03:25,500
but as an adult

143
00:03:25,533 --> 00:03:26,700
when some red envelopes arrive

144
00:03:26,766 --> 00:03:28,266
your heart tightens up first
